  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 AM Tuesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... This morning will be the coolest morning of this \nstretch with one last day of dry weather and less humid conditions \nbefore humid weather returns later this week. \n\nThe morning surface analysis shows a surface trough moving across \nCentral NC early this morning with a modest surge of drier air and \nlower dewpoints moving into the area from the northwest. In fact, \nmorning low level thickness values this morning at 12Z will be about \n10m cooler than yesterday. Lingering low level moisture confined to \nthe southern Coastal Plain, where dew points are still in the lower \n70s, has supported the development of fog, mainly across Sampson, \nWayne and Edgecombe Counties. This fog should be eroded from the \nwest by the arrival of drier air early this morning and if \nstill present, it should be burned off after sunrise.\n\nMainly clear skies are expect today and tonight although there may \nbe a few wisps of high cirrus clouds and possibly a few clouds fair \nweather clouds. No convection is expected. Highs today should range \nin the mid to upper 80s which is near if not a degree or two above \naverage. Lows tonight will range in the 60s. Winds will be light \nalthough a brief gust or two is possible after daybreak through mid \nmorning as the surface inversion breaks and the shallow low level \njet mixes to the surface. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... Diurnally maximized convection returns to central \nNC as early as Wednesday, but more likely Thursday into Saturday \nbefore shifting towards the coast through the weekend.\n\nA broad mid/upper lvl trough will persist over the northeastern \nCONUS on Wed before gradually sharpening Wed night into Thurs as a \npair of shortwave troughs carousel across the Upper Great Lakes and \nsouthern Ontario through Thurs night. At the surface, weak high \npressure, progged to be located over the northern Mid-Atlantic Wed \nmorning, is expected to shift offshore by Wed afternoon and return \ninitially easterly, then southerly flow to the Carolinas Wed. \nInitially, moisture return will be quite shallow with 70s dew points \nin the morning mixing out into the 60s through the afternoon, \nespecially over the eastern Piedmont. Isolated to scattered \nafternoon showers remain possible Wed, but likely organized around \nthe inland spreading seabreeze and the NC mountains, where southerly \nflow and terrain circulations could provide surface forcing within \nweakly unstable air mass. \n\nWed night into Fri: Continued sharpening of the slow-moving trough \naxis to our west is expected with deep layer moisture returning to \nnear-normal values, but increasingly perturbed from upstream \nconvection. This will favor increasing cloud cover and \nshowers/storms possible along the track of any disturbance. \nDestabilization will be a tricky forecast challenge and will likely \nimpact the coverage and intensity of showers/storms each day. \nHowever, a favorable pattern for repeated rounds of heavy rain \nappears possible in this window with the slow-moving and sharpening \ntrough to the east, persistent southwesterly moisture advection, and \nsaturated and perturbed lower/mid tropospheric flow. As a result, \nexcessive rain remains possible Thurs and perhaps more probable on \nFri as low-lvl convergence from an approaching front and enhanced \nand stationary moisture transport persists over the Coastal Plain of \nthe Carolinas.",
